# pens
?if b1.vow == "animal"
  The Forum Boarium is a rough, wet, bellowing place at that hour. You buy the blood of three heifers from a butcher who does not ask why, and who is paid in good coin, which is the best reason there is for not asking. You drink it standing in the yard. It is thin and flat and cold, and it fills you the way water fills a stomach. You are not quite satisfied. You are, however, still a person.
  $ do feed("animal")
  $ do earn(-3)
  $ humanity += 1
?elif b1.vow == "donor"
  The cup room is lit, and the laundress sits on the bench with her sleeve already rolled. She is cheerful. She tells you about her daughter. You take what you need, and you do not take more, and when you are done she pats your hand as though you were a nervous horse.
  $ do feed("donor")
  $ bond.prisca += 1
?else
  You go to a part of the city that you know a little better than you would like, and you find what you need. It is quick, and it is quiet, and you walk home afterward through the dark with the whole of your body singing. It is a very good feeling. You are quite aware of what you are going to have to answer for.
  $ do feed("human")
  $ secrecy -= 1
?end
<~

# tiro_hunt
?if b1.vow == "animal"
  Tiro has never hunted an animal in his life. He insists on it being a surprise. It is a very small surprise, and it is a hen, and it is in the yard of a tavern on the Via Lata. He goes in with a profound, theatrical caution, and a dozen outraged birds come up in a cloud, and he comes out with a feather in his hair and a look of extreme dignity. "I got one," he says, and he has. You both agree that it is the worst thing either of you has ever eaten. You do not stop laughing till dawn.
  $ bond.tiro += 3
  $ do feed("animal")
  $ humanity += 1
?elif b1.vow == "donor"
  Tiro comes to the cup room with you, and sits on the bench, and watches with a candid, uneasy interest. "I never go," he says. "I do not like the way they look at you." The boy with the bandaged arm, who has been there all month, says, quite gently, "They look at you because you are the one who comes." Tiro does not say anything for a while.
  $ bond.tiro += 2
  $ do feed("donor")
?else
  Tiro does not come. He has said that he will not, with a rueful, flat politeness that you cannot argue with. He wants to know where you are going, and when you will be back, and he wants you to know that he has asked. It is a very small thing. It feels like a door being closed a little way.
  $ bond.tiro -= 2
  $ do feed("human")
?end
<~

# prisca_word
Prisca receives you in the counting room with her tablets in front of her. She has already been told. She has, you suspect, been told by three different people, and she is not surprised by any of them.
?if b1.vow == "animal"
  "A house that drinks from cattle," says Prisca, in her dry, unhurried voice, "is a house of farmers. I have no objection. I have been a farmer. It is a good life, and nobody comes to your door at midnight." She turns a bead. "I will tell you what it costs. You will be slower. You will be hungrier. You will be, I am quite sure, a worse fighter, for as long as you keep it, and the Senate counts fighters. If it is a thing you can bear, I will say that I admire it. I will not say that I understand it."
  $ standing -= 1
  $ bond.prisca += 2
?elif b1.vow == "donor"
  "The cup," says Prisca, "is the house's oldest bargain, and I am glad to see it used. It is the best arrangement the made have found. It will cost you a little, in the matter of looking over your shoulder at the people who are fond of you." She smiles, very slightly. "I do not say it is easy. I say that it is sustainable. That is a better word."
  $ bond.prisca += 2
  $ standing += 1
?else
  "I will not tell you what to eat," says Prisca. "I have never told anybody. I will tell you what it costs. Every man you take is a man the city may notice. Every one that you do not hide is a sum on the Senate's book, and the Senate keeps a long memory, and a good clerk." She looks at you. "You will be strong, I grant you. Strength is a debt that is paid in noise."
  $ secrecy -= 1
  $ bond.prisca -= 1
?end
She says no more of it. She asks about the firm instead, and Hanno, who has been sitting very still in the corner, supplies the figures, and for a few minutes it is as though it were an ordinary night in an ordinary counting house. It is, you think, the kindest thing she could do.
<~

# test
## The test
$ hunger += 25
$ unflag b1.relapse
?if b1.vow == "human"
  -> the_house_watches
?end
It is the fourth night. You have been careful, and the hunger has been careful too, a thin coiled thing that waits at the edge of the lamp. You are walking home across the Forum Boarium at a very late hour with your hands in your sleeves.
The city is not empty. A man comes out of an alley ahead of you, quite suddenly, with one arm held across his chest. He is a drover, or a porter, or something of the kind, in a heavy tunic, and a thing has gone wrong with him: a fall, perhaps, or a fight. There is a long deep cut down the inside of his arm, and it is bleeding, quite fast, onto the stones.
He sees you. He holds out the good hand. "Please," he says. "Please. I can't make it stop."
There is nobody else on the street. There is nobody for a hundred paces. The smell of him comes to you like a hand on the face.
:the_drover
* Bind the arm, and take him to a physician
  $ humanity += 3
  $ standing += 1
  $ unflag b1.relapse
  You do it. You do it with your own cloak, and a belt, and a will you did not know you had. You half carry him to a physician in the Vicus Tuscus, who wakes in a temper and softens when he sees the arm, and you stand in the lamplit doorway while the man is stitched, and every drop of blood on the floor is a small bright wound in your own head. You do not take it. You do not take a single drop. When you go out, the dawn is paling, and you are shaking all over, and you have never in your life been so proud, or so tired, or so hungry.
* Drink
  $ b1.relapse = true
  $ do feed("human")
  $ humanity -= 3
  $ secrecy -= 1
  It is quick. It is almost easy. It is the easiest thing you have done in a month, and it is the worst, because it is the one that tells you how little your will is worth. You stop before the end, and you bind the arm, and you leave him sitting in the lamplight in a daze. You walk home. You tell yourself that it will not happen again. You are, you notice, no longer very good at telling yourself things.
* Cross the street, and walk on
  $ humanity -= 1
  $ cunning_brutal += 1
  You cross the street. His voice follows you, small and desperate, until the corner. You do not look back. You know that someone will find him. You know that someone probably will. It is not a thing you will tell anyone, and it is not a thing you will forget.
-> ending

# the_house_watches
## A word from Hanno
You are crossing the cellar in the small hours when Hanno steps out of the dark by the stair. He has not made a sound. He has a tablet in one hand and a small lamp in the other.
"A river man," he says, "on the quay at the Emporium. And a drover at the cattle market. And a woman who sells cabbages in the Subura, who has a bite on her neck, and who is not at all sure how it came there." He sets the lamp on a jar. "I do not ask. I note. I would ask you to consider that the Lictors can count, too."
He says it quite gently. It is the longest speech he has made to you since the ledgers.
$ secrecy -= 1
$ standing -= 1
:the_note
* Promise to be more careful
  $ secrecy += 2
  $ cunning_brutal -= 1
  He inclines his head, with great dignity, and goes on up the stair.
* Tell him that it is your business
  $ defiance += 1
  $ bond.prisca -= 1
  "It is," says Hanno. "I merely note that it is also the house's." He does not look angry. He looks like a man marking a figure in a column he intends to return to.
-> ending

# ending
## Before the dark of the moon
$ do rest()
?if b1.vow == "animal" and not b1.relapse
  Weeks pass, and the hunger does not go away. It becomes something you carry, like a stone in a boot, a thing that you learn to walk on. You are slower. You are thinner, in some way that does not show. You are, quite oddly, calmer, and Tiro says that you smile more than you did.
?elif b1.vow == "animal" and b1.relapse
  Weeks pass, and you do not speak of the drover. Tiro does not ask. You keep your vow, in the sense that you make it again every dawn, and some mornings you believe it a little less, and some mornings you believe it more. It is a very odd kind of faith, and you have begun to think that it may be the only kind there is.
?elif b1.vow == "donor" and not b1.relapse
  The cup becomes a habit, and then a custom, and then, rather to your surprise, a friendship. The laundress's daughter has been brought to the counting house, to learn her letters, and she is very quick.
?elif b1.vow == "donor"
  You go on with the cup. You do not go to the Forum Boarium after dark, and Tiro goes with you, as if by accident, every night.
?else
  You go on as before. The Forum Boarium sees you less than it did. The rest of Rome sees you rather more, and you walk through it with a lighter step and a heavier name, and you do not often look at what is behind you.
?end
It is the day before the dark of the moon when Prisca sends for you. She does not ask you to the roof this time. She asks you to the counting room, with the shutters closed, the lamp turned low, and Hanno at the table with the ledger open to a page you have not seen.
"The house has been very patient with you," says Prisca. "I have been very patient. I should like, I think, to ask you now what you mean to be."
She says it without any weight at all.
"Think about it. The dark of the moon is in two nights. I shall want your answer then."
